  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA blocking high anchored over the Great Lakes through tonight \nwill keep central Illinois quiet under seasonable conditions, \nwith high temperatures in the mid-70s and lows near 50. The \nregion remains situated between an upper-level shortwave trough \nmoving inland from the eastern Pacific and an east coast low off\nNew England.\n\nBy Tuesday, the synoptic setup rapidly changes as the western \ntrough absorbs the remnants of Tropical Depression Polo. \nShortwave energy downstream will eject across the Plains into \nthe Mid- and Upper-Mississippi Valleys, pushing the east coast \nlow out to sea and dismantling the blocking ridge. Associated \nthermal and moisture advection will drive afternoon temperatures\ninto the 80s for Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nRecent model guidance has pushed back precipitation timing. \nTrends show the parent trough phasing with Polo and forming a \nclosed upper low. This will hold the surface cold front to our \nnorthwest, delaying rainfall chances across central Illinois \nuntil early Thursday morning.\n\nWhile some uncertainty remains concerning the cutoff low, model\nguidance consistently shows it opening up and tracking eastward\nalong the slow-moving cold front by Friday. High confidence \nexists for widespread rainfall from Thursday through Friday; \nhowever, exact accumulation totals and localized mesoscale \nbanding will depend on the timing and sharpness of the front. \nBoth blended and ensemble guidance suggest a 40-60% probability \nof rainfall exceeding 1 inch north of Interstate 70 through \nFriday evening. The likelihood of total accumulations exceeding \n2 inches is lower (10-30%), though localized mesoscale banding \ncould still produce these higher amounts.\n\nAlthough low precipitation chances persist through midday \nSaturday to account for a potential delay in the northern stream\nshortwave and frontal boundary, conditions are expected to dry \nout as Continental Polar (cP) high pressure rapidly expands \nacross the Midwest and Great Lakes region. This airmass shift \nwill usher in a distinct cooling trend relative to mid-week \ntemperatures, holding daily highs in the upper 60s as the week \ndraws to a close. Extended guidance suggests this high-pressure \nsystem will remain entrenched through midweek, maintaining a \nstretch of ideal autumn weather.",
